-- title:
--   Histories, predictable processes and the stochastic intensity
-- statement:
--   The vocabulary of §§1.8-1.9.
--
--   A **history** $\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ is an increasing family of sub-$\sigma$-fields
--   of $\mathcal{F}$; it is a history **of** a point process $N$ when $N$ is adapted to it. Write
--   $\mathcal{F}_{0-} = \bigvee_{s<0}\mathcal{F}_s$.
--
--   The history is *compatible* with the flow when $\theta_t\mathcal{F}_s = \mathcal{F}_{s-t}$, and if
--   in addition any $\{\mathcal{F}_t\}$-predictable process $\{Z(t)\}$ has the form
--   $$ Z(t,\omega) = v(t, \theta_t\omega) , \tag{1.8.1} $$
--   with $v$ being $\mathcal{B}\otimes\mathcal{F}$ measurable and, for all $t$, $\omega \mapsto
--   v(t,\omega)$ being $\mathcal{F}_{0-}$-measurable, then $\mathcal{F}_t$ is said to have a
--   **predictable structure adapted to $\{\theta_t\}$**.
--
--   Two $\sigma$-fields on $\mathbb{R}\times\Omega$ make that precise and are what Theorem 1.8.1 is
--   about: the **predictable** $\sigma$-field $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}_t)$, generated by the rectangles
--   $(a,b] \times A$ with $A \in \mathcal{F}_a$, and $\tau(\mathcal{F}_t)$, generated by the mappings
--   of the form (1.8.2).
--
--   The **internal history** $\{\mathcal{F}^{N,Z}_t\}$ is the smallest history to which $N$ and its
--   marks are adapted; it is characterised by that minimality rather than constructed.
--
--   $N$ admits the **$(P,\mathcal{F}_t)$-intensity** $\{\lambda(t)\}$ when $\{\lambda(t)\}$ is
--   non-negative and $\mathcal{F}_t$-adapted and, for all $a \le b$ and $A \in \mathcal{F}_a$,
--   $$ E[N(a,b]\mathbf{1}_A] = E\Big[\Big(\int_a^b \lambda(t)dt\Big)\mathbf{1}_A\Big] , $$
--   which is the form Remark 1.9.3 writes as (1.9.1). The **constant** $\mathcal{F}_t$-intensity is the
--   special case $E[N(a,b] \mid \mathcal{F}_a] = \lambda(b-a)$, which is PASTA's hypothesis.
-- source:
--   Baccelli & Bremaud, Elements of Queueing Theory: Palm Martingale Calculus and Stochastic Recurrences, 2nd ed., Springer 2003, §§1.8-1.9, pp. 55-66

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PalmQueueing_Palm_PointProcess

/-!
# Histories, predictable processes and the stochastic intensity (§§1.8-1.9, pp.55-64)

The vocabulary of Chapter 1's last two sections, in the `PalmQueueing.Palm` namespace with the rest
of the Chapter 1 substrate because that is what it is. Two missions of this series need it — the
one that states Theorems 1.8.1-1.9.3 and the one that states PASTA — and a second private copy
would let them drift apart.

The one that carries the theorem is **predictability**. PASTA is false for an arbitrary
`θ_t`-compatible process: an arrival that changes the state it then observes sees something other
than the time average. What rules that out is that `{Z(t)}` be `F_t`-**predictable** — measurable
with respect to the strict past. Theorem 1.8.1 says the internal history of a marked point process
has a *predictable structure adapted to `{θ_t}`*, meaning every predictable process has the form

`(1.8.1)  Z(t, ω) = v(t, θ_t ω)`

with `v` jointly measurable and `ω ↦ v(t, ω)` `F_{0−}`-measurable for each `t`. That form is used
here as the definition, because it is concrete, it is what the proof of PASTA consumes, and it is
what Theorem 1.8.1 guarantees is available.
-/

namespace PalmQueueing.Palm

open MeasureTheory Filter Topology

variable {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]

/-- A **history** `{F_t}_{t ∈ ℝ}` on `(Ω, F)` (§1.8, p.55): an increasing family of sub-σ-fields. -/
structure History (Ω : Type*) [MeasurableSpace Ω] where
  /-- The σ-field of the past up to `t`. -/
  F : ℝ → MeasurableSpace Ω
  /-- It increases with `t`. -/
  mono : ∀ s t : ℝ, s ≤ t → F s ≤ F t
  /-- Each is a sub-σ-field of `F`. -/
  le_top : ∀ t : ℝ, F t ≤ ‹MeasurableSpace Ω›

/-- `F_{0−} = ⋁_{s < 0} F_s` (p.57): the strict past of the origin. -/
noncomputable def History.zeroMinus (H : History Ω) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ s : {s : ℝ // s < 0}, H.F (s : ℝ)

/-- `{F_t}` is a **history of `N`** (§1.8, p.55): the point process is adapted to it. -/
def History.IsHistoryOf (H : History Ω) (N : PointProcess Ω) : Prop :=
  ∀ a b : ℝ, a ≤ b → Measurable[H.F b] fun ω => (N.count ω (Set.Ioc a b)).toReal

/-- `{Z(t)}` is **`F_t`-predictable**, in the form (1.8.1) that a history with a predictable
structure adapted to `{θ_t}` gives (p.57): `Z(t, ω) = v(t, θ_t ω)` where `v` is `B ⊗ F`-measurable
and, for each `t`, `ω ↦ v(t, ω)` is `F_{0−}`-measurable.

The `F_{0−}`-measurability of each `v(t, ·)` is the whole content: it says the value observed at
`t` is determined by the strict past, which is what an arriving customer can see and what a
customer that *causes* a change cannot. Dropping it makes PASTA false. -/
def IsPredictable {K : Type*} [MeasurableSpace K] (H : History Ω) (θ : Flow Ω)
    (Z : ℝ → Ω → K) : Prop :=
  ∃ v : ℝ → Ω → K,
    (Measurable fun p : ℝ × Ω => v p.1 p.2) ∧
    (∀ t : ℝ, Measurable[H.zeroMinus] (v t)) ∧
    ∀ (t : ℝ) (ω : Ω), Z t ω = v t (θ t ω)

/-- `N` admits the **constant `F_t`-intensity `λ`** (§1.8.2, p.61, and the hypothesis of PASTA):
for all `a ≤ b`, `E[ N(a,b] | F_a ] = λ (b − a)`.

Remark 3.3.1 records that, by Watanabe's theorem, `A` admits a constant `F_t`-intensity `λ` if and
only if it is a Poisson process with rate `λ` **and** `A(a,b]` is independent of `F_a` for all
`(a,b] ⊂ ℝ`. That equivalence is a remark on the page, not the hypothesis; substituting "`A` is
Poisson" for this definition would import a theorem this mission has not stated. -/
def HasConstantIntensity (H : History Ω) (N : PointProcess Ω) (P : Measure Ω) (lam : ℝ) : Prop :=
  0 < lam ∧
  ∀ a b : ℝ, a ≤ b →
    condExp (H.F a) P (fun ω => (N.count ω (Set.Ioc a b)).toReal)
      =ᵐ[P] fun _ => lam * (b - a)

/-- `N` admits the **`(P, F_t)`-intensity `{λ(t)}`** (§1.8, p.59): `{λ(t)}` is non-negative and
`F_t`-adapted, and for all `a ≤ b` and all `A ∈ F_a`,

`E[ N(a,b] 1_A ] = E[ (∫_a^b λ(t)dt) 1_A ]`.

This is the form Remark 1.9.3 writes the conclusion of Theorem 1.9.1 in, as `(1.9.1)`, and the one
Theorem 1.9.2 characterises. Everything is in `ℝ≥0∞`, so no integrability hypothesis is smuggled
in. -/
def HasIntensity (H : History Ω) (N : PointProcess Ω) (P : Measure Ω)
    (lamProc : ℝ → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ (t : ℝ) (ω : Ω), 0 ≤ lamProc t ω) ∧
  (∀ t : ℝ, Measurable[H.F t] (lamProc t)) ∧
  ∀ a b : ℝ, a ≤ b → ∀ A : Set Ω, MeasurableSet[H.F a] A →
    ∫⁻ ω, Set.indicator A (fun _ => (1 : ENNReal)) ω * N.count ω (Set.Ioc a b) ∂P
      = ∫⁻ ω, Set.indicator A (fun _ => (1 : ENNReal)) ω *
          ENNReal.ofReal (∫ t in Set.Ioc a b, lamProc t ω ∂(volume : Measure ℝ)) ∂P

/-- The **predictable σ-field** `P(F_t)` on `ℝ × Ω` (p.57): generated by the rectangles
`(a,b] × A` with `A ∈ F_a`. This is the σ-field the proof of Theorem 1.8.1 works with, through the
processes `X(t,ω) = H(ω)1_{(a,b]}(t)` with `H` `F_a`-measurable. -/
def History.predictableSigma (H : History Ω) : MeasurableSpace (ℝ × Ω) :=
  MeasurableSpace.generateFrom
    {S | ∃ (a b : ℝ) (A : Set Ω), MeasurableSet[H.F a] A ∧ S = Set.Ioc a b ×ˢ A}

/-- `τ(F_t)`, the σ-field on `ℝ × Ω` generated by the mappings of the form

`(1.8.2)  X(t, ω) = v(t, θ_t ω)`,

where `v` is `B ⊗ F`-measurable and such that for each `t ∈ ℝ`, `ω ↦ v(t, ω)` is
`F_{0−}`-measurable (p.57). -/
def History.shiftSigma (H : History Ω) (θ : Flow Ω) : MeasurableSpace (ℝ × Ω) :=
  ⨆ v : {v : ℝ → Ω → ℝ //
      (Measurable fun p : ℝ × Ω => v p.1 p.2) ∧ ∀ t : ℝ, Measurable[H.zeroMinus] (v t)},
    MeasurableSpace.comap (fun p : ℝ × Ω => (v : ℝ → Ω → ℝ) p.1 (θ p.1 p.2)) inferInstance

/-- `N` and its marks `{Z_n}` are **adapted** to the history `{F_t}`: for every Borel `C ⊆ (−∞,t]`
and every measurable `L`, the count `N_L(C) = #{n : T_n ∈ C, Z_n ∈ L}` is `F_t`-measurable. -/
def History.MarkedAdapted {K : Type*} [MeasurableSpace K] (H : History Ω) (N : PointProcess Ω)
    (Z : ℤ → Ω → K) : Prop :=
  ∀ (t : ℝ) (C : Set ℝ), MeasurableSet C → C ⊆ Set.Iic t → ∀ L : Set K, MeasurableSet L →
    Measurable[H.F t] fun ω => ∑' n : ℤ,
      Set.indicator C (fun _ => (1 : ENNReal)) (N.T n ω) *
      Set.indicator L (fun _ => (1 : ENNReal)) (Z n ω)

/-- `{F_t}` is the **internal history** `{F_t^{N,Z}}` of `(N, {Z_n})` (§1.8, p.55): the smallest
history to which `N` and its marks are adapted.

Characterised rather than constructed: what Theorem 1.8.1 needs is minimality, and building the
σ-field explicitly would add an object the book does not use. -/
def History.IsInternalOf {K : Type*} [MeasurableSpace K] (H : History Ω) (N : PointProcess Ω)
    (Z : ℤ → Ω → K) : Prop :=
  H.MarkedAdapted N Z ∧
    ∀ H' : History Ω, H'.MarkedAdapted N Z → ∀ t : ℝ, H.F t ≤ H'.F t

end PalmQueueing.Palm


